<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Six Nations of the Grand River</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1784">1784</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Six Nations Reserve</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Six Nations of the Grand River is the most populous First Nations reserve in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, near Brantford in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>, home to members of all six nations of the Iroquois Confederacy. It derives from land along the Grand River granted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haldimand-proclamation">Haldimand Proclamation</a> of 1784 to Haudenosaunee allies of the British, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-brant">Joseph Brant</a>, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>. The original grant was later much reduced.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/six-nations-of-the-grand-river.jpg" alt="Six Nations of the Grand River" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Terfili · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Place_sign,_Six_Nations,_Ontario.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The reserve lies near Brantford, bordered by the County of Brant, Norfolk County and Haldimand County, with the New Credit Reserve inside its bounds. It covers some 46,000 acres, about 8% of the 550,000 acres of the original grant along the Grand River. Its people belong to the six nations of the Haudenosaunee Confederacy, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a>, Cayuga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onondaga-people">Onondaga</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oneida-people">Oneida</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a> and Tuscarora, and some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a> live there too. Its communities include Ohsweken, Medina Corners, Smoothtown and Sour Spring.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a> the Crown ceded the lands of the Six Nations in the colonies without consulting them. Sir Frederick Haldimand settled John Deseronto's Mohawk of the Lower Castle on the Bay of Quinte, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-brant">Joseph Brant</a> chose the Grand River, with the Mohawk of the Upper Castle and bands of the other nations. The land had been bought from the Mississauga on 22 May 1784. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1785 counted 1,843 Natives on the reserve, including 548 Mohawk, and 400 people from other peoples. Brant also brought enslaved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> and invited veterans of Brant's Volunteers and Butler's Rangers to settle, and from the 1830s to the 1860s many people escaping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/underground-railroad">Underground Railroad</a> joined the community.</p><p>In 1785 the government built Her Majesty's Royal Chapel of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawks</a>, the first Protestant church in Upper Canada. The chiefs rejected Governor John Simcoe's limited deed of 14 January 1793 and Lord Dorchester's of 1796. Between 1795 and 1797 Brant sold 381,480 acres to land speculators, the northern half of the reserve going for £85,332, but by 1801 every speculator had fallen behind in payments. Disputes over those sales lie behind the Grand River land dispute. Children of the reserve later attended the Mohawk Institute, a residential school that was the subject of numerous abuse allegations until it closed in 1972.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In March 2023 the reserve had 28,520 band members, 11,688 of whom lived on it, making it the most populous reserve in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. Its government recognised by Canada is the Six Nations of the Grand River Elected Council, set up under the Indian Act in 1924, while the Haudenosaunee Confederacy Chiefs Council continues the traditional government of the Confederacy on the reserve.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Six Nations of the Grand River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the Haldimand Proclamation of 1784, Brant led many loyalist Haudenosaunee to the Grand River in present-day Ontario, where the <b>Six Nations of the Grand River</b> is now the most populous First Nations reserve in Canada.</blockquote><a href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Six Nations of the Grand River</b> is the largest First Nations reserve in Canada, and the Grand Council still exists.</blockquote><a href="/culture/haudenosaunee">Haudenosaunee · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Today the Tuscarora Nation lives near Lewiston, New York, and at <b>Six Nations of the Grand River</b> in Ontario.</blockquote><a href="/state/tuscarora-nation">Tuscarora Nation</a></li></ul></section>
